<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huneric</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned 477–<a href="/years/484">484</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huneric was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, son and successor of Gaiseric. As a young man he had been a hostage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and he married Eudocia, daughter of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii">Valentinian III</a>. A committed Arian, in 484 he launched the harshest persecution of Nicene Christians under Vandal rule, exiling many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and clergy. He died later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huneric.jpg" alt="Huneric" width="263" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Courtesy of Asta Tintinna Numismatica and deamoneta.com · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huneric_BMC_012_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gaiseric styled himself King of the Vandals and Alans and promulgated a law of succession by which the oldest male of the royal house was to succeed; it carried the crown to his son <b>Huneric</b> in 477, then to Huneric's cousin Gunthamund, to Thrasamund, Hilderic and Gelimer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After a meeting of Nicene and Arian bishops earlier that month, <b>Huneric</b> forcibly removed the Nicene bishops from office and banished some to Corsica.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-24">24 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
